<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Shapes Around Us – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Shapes Around Us</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> That Is a Circle.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Recognize familiar words in simple texts. Reading: Match simple words to corresponding pictures. Phonemic Awareness: Identify sounds of the letters of the alphabet when supported with a concrete item or picture representing that sound.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can point to pictures of shapes when prompted during reading activities (e.g., pointing to a picture of a triangle in a storybook when asked, "What is this shape?"). Can match simple shape-related words to pictures (e.g., "square," "circle"). Can produce the sound of the alphabet letters when supported by a concrete item or picture representing the sound (e.g., /s/ in "square").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students with "Good morning, shapes explorers!" and shows colorful flashcards of shapes (circle, square, triangle, rectangle, oval, diamond, star). The teacher says the name of each shape and students repeat chorally (e.g., "Circle!"). The teacher then points to objects in the classroom and asks, "What shape is this?" or "Is this a square?" to review previously learned vocabulary and activate prior knowledge. For example, pointing to a round clock and saying, "Look! That is a circle!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard with the word "circle" clearly written below the picture of a circle. The teacher points to the word "circle" and reads it aloud, emphasizing the initial sound /s/. The teacher repeats this for "square," "rectangle," "triangle," pointing to each word and modeling the pronunciation, especially /s/ in "square" and /r/ in "rectangle". The teacher also models "That is a circle." while pointing to the word.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing the word "square") "Is this word 'circle'?" (Students shake heads or say "No" / "Square!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing the word "rectangle" and its picture) "What shape is this word for?" (Students point to the rectangle picture or say "Rectangle!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Shapes Around Us".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher presents a large, colorful "Shape Book" or a projected simple story with short sentences and large, clear pictures. The material is a short narrative excerpt with pictures illustrating shapes in everyday objects.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> "Our Shape Book" – a simple picture book or projected slides with sentences like:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Look! That is a circle." (Picture of a sun)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I see a square. That is a square table." (Picture of a square table)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"This is a triangle." (Picture of a pizza slice)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"It is a rectangle." (Picture of a door)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What shape is it? It is an oval!" (Picture of an egg)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Recognize familiar words in simple texts. Reading: Match simple words to corresponding pictures. Phonemic Awareness: Identify sounds of the letters of the alphabet when supported with a concrete item or picture representing that sound.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What are we reading about?" (Shapes!). The teacher guides students to point to shapes. "Show me the circle!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching:</b> The teacher reads a sentence (e.g., "That is a circle.") and asks students to point to the word "circle" in the sentence, then point to the picture of the circle. The teacher then provides individual flashcards with shape words and asks students to match them to the correct pictures in the book.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Identifying initial sounds:</b> As the teacher points to "square" in the text, she asks, "What sound does 'square' start with?" (/s/). She repeats this for "rectangle" (/r/).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading words for shapes (square, rectangle, circle, triangle, oval, diamond, star), simple sentences using "That is a..." and "This is a...", recognizing initial sounds (/s/, /r/). Grammar: Shape + noun (e.g., "a square table").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice (Word-Picture Matching):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with simple shape pictures on one side and the corresponding shape words (circle, square, triangle, rectangle, oval, diamond, star) written on the other side in a mixed order. Students draw lines to match each word to its correct picture. The teacher circulates, providing support and checking for accuracy, prompting students to say the word as they match. "Match 'circle' to the circle!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Reading & Drawing):</b> The teacher projects or writes simple sentences on the board: "That is a circle." "This is a square." "I see a rectangle." Students read each sentence chorally or individually. After reading, they draw the shape described in the sentence in their notebooks or on a mini-whiteboard. The teacher focuses on word recognition for "circle," "square," "rectangle."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair Reading & Recognition:</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of flashcards with shape words (e.g., "circle," "square"). One student holds up a word card, and the other student reads the word aloud and then points to a real object in the classroom that has that shape. (e.g., Student A holds "square," Student B reads "square" and points to a square window). They then switch roles. Teacher prompts: "What did you read? Show me the shape!"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Shapes Around Us".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task: "Read and Find the Shape!"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students receive a worksheet with 4-5 simple sentences describing objects with shapes they've learned, each followed by three small pictures (one correct, two distractors). For example:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. "That is a round sun. It is a circle." (Picture of sun, tree, house)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. "I see a big square box." (Picture of a box, a ball, a book)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. "This is a long rectangle door." (Picture of a door, a cup, a star)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. "It is a triangle pizza." (Picture of pizza, a fish, a car)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students read each sentence independently, then circle the picture that matches the description. They can work individually or in small groups (2-3 students) to help each other read and identify. The teacher encourages students to point to the shape word in the sentence before circling the picture. The teacher asks students to softly say the initial sound of the shape word as they identify it. For example, pointing to "sun" and saying "s-s-s-sun, circle!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment: Reading Recognition Quiz</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students receive a short quiz sheet. The quiz has two sections:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Word to Picture Match:</b> A list of 4-5 shape words (e.g., circle, square, triangle, rectangle) and a corresponding set of pictures in a different order. Students draw lines to match the word to its correct picture.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Read and Point/Circle:</b> Two simple sentences (e.g., "That is a star." "This is a diamond."). Below each sentence are two pictures. Students read the sentence and circle the correct picture.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher observes students as they complete the quiz, noting their ability to recognize words and match them. She also listens for students' ability to produce the initial sound of the letters of the alphabet when pointing to the shape words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correctly matches shape words to pictures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognizes familiar shape words in simple sentences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can identify (produce) the initial sound of the letter in shape names (e.g., /s/ for "square") when prompted by the word.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are given a small paper slip with two emojis (happy face, neutral face). They circle the face that shows how they feel about reading shape words. Below it, they complete: "I can read the word: _________ (e.g., circle)."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What new shape word can you read today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Was it easy or difficult to match the words to the pictures?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this reading lesson? (e.g., specific activities, materials, student engagement). The picture-word matching activities were very effective for Pre-A1.3 learners. Students enjoyed pointing to and reading words for classroom objects.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson (Speaking)? Some students still need more practice recognizing words quickly. For the next speaking lesson, I will ensure clear visual prompts and sentence frames are available to support their oral production, building on the words they can now read. I will incorporate more choral repetition before individual speaking tasks.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson, students will use these shape words and simple sentences ("That is a circle," "This is a square table") to actively describe and talk about shapes they see around them and in pictures.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>